# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Numbers'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'law'
SUBTITLE = 'Wilderness Journey, Unbelief, and the Promise Held'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Numbers follows Israel from Sinai toward the land. It numbers the tribes, orders the camp around the tabernacle, and sets the Levites apart. It then records the people’s murmuring, the report of the spies and the refusal to go up, the forty years in the wilderness, the bronze serpent, Balaam’s blessings, the second numbering, Joshua’s appointment, and the borders of the land (Num. 1:1–3; 14:11; 21:8–9; 24:17; 27:18; 34:2).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where Israel is God’s covenant nation on its way to the land of promise, with its tribes, camp, priesthood and inheritance (Num. 6:27; 34:2).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Numbers', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Camp at Sinai (Chs. 1–10)'), ('p', 'The tribes are numbered, the camp is set in order around the tabernacle, the Levites are given to the service, and the priestly blessing is given (Num. 1:1–3; 6:24–27).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Murmuring and the Spies (Chs. 11–14)'), ('p', 'The people murmur, the spies bring back their report, and the LORD says, “How long will this people provoke me?” (Num. 11:23; 13:30; 14:11, 24).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Rebellion and the Priesthood (Chs. 15–19)'), ('p', 'Korah’s rebellion is judged, and Aaron’s rod that budded confirms the priesthood (Num. 15:38; 16:1–35; 17:8).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Meribah, the Serpent, and Balaam (Chs. 20–25)'), ('p', 'Moses and Aaron are barred from the land, the bronze serpent heals the bitten, and Balaam blesses Israel instead of cursing (Num. 20:12; 21:8–9; 23:19; 24:9, 17).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — The New Generation and the Borders (Chs. 26–36)'), ('p', 'A second numbering is taken, Joshua is appointed, and the borders and the inheritance of the land are set out (Num. 27:18; 32:23; 34:2; 35:34).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Numbers', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Priestly Blessing</b> — “The LORD bless thee, and keep thee” (Num. 6:24). “They shall put my name upon the children of Israel; and I will bless them” (Num. 6:27).', '<b>Unbelief at the Border</b> — “How long will it be ere they believe me, for all the signs which I have shewed among them?” (Num. 14:11).', '<b>A Faithful Remnant</b> — “My servant Caleb, because he had another spirit with him, and hath followed me fully, him will I bring into the land” (Num. 14:24).', '<b>The Bronze Serpent</b> — “Every one that is bitten, when he looketh upon it, shall live” (Num. 21:8).', '<b>God Does Not Lie</b> — “God is not a man, that he should lie” (Num. 23:19).', '<b>A Star and a Sceptre</b> — “There shall come a Star out of Jacob, and a Sceptre shall rise out of Israel” (Num. 24:17).', '<b>The Inheritance</b> — “When ye come into the land of Canaan; (this is the land that shall fall unto you for an inheritance)” (Num. 34:2).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Numbers', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Numbers points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Lifted Serpent</b> — The serpent lifted on a pole is the figure the Lord uses of Himself (Num. 21:8–9; John 3:14–15).', '<b>The Star out of Jacob</b> — Balaam’s words are reported as written: a Star out of Jacob and a Sceptre out of Israel (Num. 24:17).', '<b>The Priestly Blessing</b> — The blessing of Aaron’s sons puts the LORD’s name upon Israel (Num. 6:24–27).', '<b>The Faithful Servant</b> — Caleb followed fully, and Israel’s later Joshua is appointed to lead them in (Num. 14:24; 27:18).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Numbers', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Numbers speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Numbers operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Numbers in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Numbers reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['tribal order and camp around the tabernacle (Num. 1:1–3)', 'priestly blessing and the LORD’s name upon the children of Israel (Num. 6:24–27)', 'inheritance and its borders (Num. 34:2)', 'call to take possession of the land (Num. 13:30; 33:53)', 'covenant with the God who does not lie (Num. 23:19)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Numbers that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Numbers contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Tribes Numbered — The LORD speaks to Moses in the tabernacle and the congregation is numbered (Num. 1:1–3).', 'The Priestly Blessing — “The LORD bless thee, and keep thee.” (Num. 6:24–26).', 'The Name Upon Israel — “they shall put my name upon the children of Israel; and I will bless them.” (Num. 6:27).', 'Unbelief — “How long will it be ere they believe me, for all the signs which I have shewed among them?” (Num. 14:11).', 'Caleb Followed Fully — “he had another spirit with him, and hath followed me fully.” (Num. 14:24).', 'Barred from the Land — “Because ye believed me not… ye shall not bring this congregation into the land.” (Num. 20:12).', 'The Bronze Serpent — “every one that is bitten, when he looketh upon it, shall live.” (Num. 21:8–9).', 'Balaam’s Blessing — “Blessed is he that blesseth thee, and cursed is he that curseth thee.” (Num. 24:9).', 'A Star out of Jacob — “there shall come a Star out of Jacob, and a Sceptre shall rise out of Israel.” (Num. 24:17).', 'Joshua Appointed — “Take thee Joshua the son of Nun, a man in whom is the spirit.” (Num. 27:18).', 'The Land of Canaan — “When ye come into the land of Canaan.” (Num. 34:2).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Numbers Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Numbers and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Numbers teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Numbers', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Tribes Numbered', 'The LORD speaks to Moses in the tabernacle and the congregation is numbered.', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 1:1–3'], ['The Priestly Blessing', '“The LORD bless thee, and keep thee.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 6:24–26'], ['The Name Upon Israel', '“they shall put my name upon the children of Israel; and I will bless them.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 6:27'], ['Unbelief', '“How long will it be ere they believe me, for all the signs which I have shewed among them?”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 14:11'], ['Caleb Followed Fully', '“he had another spirit with him, and hath followed me fully.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 14:24'], ['Barred from the Land', '“Because ye believed me not… ye shall not bring this congregation into the land.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 20:12'], ['The Bronze Serpent', '“every one that is bitten, when he looketh upon it, shall live.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 21:8–9'], ['Balaam’s Blessing', '“Blessed is he that blesseth thee, and cursed is he that curseth thee.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 24:9'], ['A Star out of Jacob', '“there shall come a Star out of Jacob, and a Sceptre shall rise out of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 24:17'], ['Joshua Appointed', '“Take thee Joshua the son of Nun, a man in whom is the spirit.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 27:18'], ['The Land of Canaan', '“When ye come into the land of Canaan.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Num. 34:2']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Numbers that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Numbers teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['The Lord’s Hand', '“Is the LORD’s hand waxed short?”', 'Num. 11:23'], ['Mercy and Justice', '“The LORD is longsuffering, and of great mercy, forgiving iniquity and transgression.”', 'Num. 14:18'], ['His Glory', '“all the earth shall be filled with the glory of the LORD.”', 'Num. 14:21'], ['God Does Not Lie', '“God is not a man, that he should lie; neither the son of man, that he should repent.”', 'Num. 23:19'], ['Sin Finds You Out', '“be sure your sin will find you out.”', 'Num. 32:23']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Numbers without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Numbers in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Trust the Lord’s power</b> — His hand is not short (Num. 11:23).', '<b>Rest in His mercy</b> — He is longsuffering and forgives (Num. 14:18).', '<b>Look for His glory</b> — The earth shall be filled with it (Num. 14:21).', '<b>Rely on His word</b> — God does not lie (Num. 23:19).', '<b>Take sin seriously</b> — It will find you out (Num. 32:23).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Numbers', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Numbers in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Camp at Sinai (Chs. 1–10) — Numbered tribes and the priestly blessing. <i>(Num. 1:1–3; 6:24–27)</i>', 'Murmuring and the Spies (Chs. 11–14) — Unbelief at the border. <i>(Num. 11:23; 14:11, 24)</i>', 'Rebellion and the Priesthood (Chs. 15–19) — Korah and Aaron’s rod. <i>(Num. 16:1–35; 17:8)</i>', 'Meribah and the Serpent (Chs. 20–21) — The barred leaders and the lifted serpent. <i>(Num. 20:12; 21:8–9)</i>', 'Balaam (Chs. 22–25) — Blessing instead of cursing. <i>(Num. 23:19; 24:9, 17)</i>', 'The New Generation (Chs. 26–31) — Numbering and Joshua. <i>(Num. 27:18)</i>', 'The Borders and the Inheritance (Chs. 32–36) — The land set out. <i>(Num. 32:23; 34:2)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Numbers, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward trust, humility, and faithfulness without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Numbers simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Numbers stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
